{"attack": true, "case": "The target's mechanism is internally contradictory. It states that acting on the best-tested theory is 'the only rational way to pursue truth,' yet simultaneously defines corroboration as 'a report card of past critical efforts rather than a predictor of future success.' If corroboration is explicitly not a predictor of future success, then preferring corroborated theories has no warranted connection to the goal of pursuing truth — the methodological rule would be a mere convention, not a rationally truth-directed strategy. The target thus claims 'procedural rationality' but the procedure it describes lacks any defensible link to truth-seeking, since by its own admission past test-survival tells us nothing about whether the theory will hold going forward. This smuggles in the very problem it claims to solve: without some minimal assumption that surviving severe tests is relevant to future performance, calling the preference 'rational' as a truth-pursuing strategy is unsupported, and the claim that it is 'the only rational way' is an overreach.", "premise": "A methodological rule can be called 'rational as a truth-seeking strategy' only if the rule's outputs bear some warranted connection to the goal of truth; a rule whose inputs are explicitly declared not to predict future success cannot be rational in that sense, only conventional.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "92cd3443ce054c750", "quote": "acting on the best-tested theory is the only rational way to pursue truth without assuming the future resembles the past, effectively treating corroboration as a report card of past critical efforts rather than a predictor of future success."}], "counterexample": ["Consider an agent who must choose between theory A (survived 100 severe tests) and theory B (survived 0 tests). On the target's own account, corroboration is 'not a predictor of future success,' so A's superior test record provides zero warrant that A is more likely to be true or to hold